{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>No hand shall touch him.</b> That no man should go up to bring him down from the mountain, for the one who ascends second is warned just like the first ascender. Rather, a court agent stands at the foot of the mountain, and the ascender — if he is close, <b>he shall be surely stoned</b> with stones; or if he is far, <b>he shall be surely shot</b>. And if you ask: why is his ascending the mountain more severe than his stoning, when stoning defiles its place? That is, if the verse was insistent about animals that they not ascend the mountain, it stands to reason that it would be even more insistent that no person be killed on the mountain to defile it. Answer to the matter: a similar thing we find in severe holiness — the Tent of Meeting, into which no stranger entered, and when Nadav and Avihu offered alien incense in it, fire came forth and consumed them and they died before HaShem, even though they defiled their place. And also in the eternal house, when Uzziah entered the Holy of Holies to burn incense, leprosy broke out on his forehead, even though he was standing in a place of severe holiness. Another interpretation: <b>for he shall be surely stoned</b> — with stones or with hail; <b>or he shall be surely shot</b> — with arrows of fire. And according to Rashi, it should have said 'for he shall be surely shot or surely stoned,' without pushing. For pushing precedes stoning, as was taught there: one of the witnesses pushes him by his hips; if he dies from it, he is exempt, and if not, he takes the stone and places it on his heart, etc. Rather, <b>or</b> here means 'which,' as in 'and he shall bring it to the door <b>or</b> to the doorpost,' which the Jerusalem Targum renders: 'and he shall bring it to the door <b>which</b> is to the doorpost.' And likewise the meaning of <b>for he shall be surely stoned — which</b> <b>he shall be surely shot</b> — first." "<b>Whether beast or man.</b> It did not mention a bird, for it cannot be held, for it would immediately fly upward." "<b>When the ram's horn sounds.</b> The truth is that it is a ram's horn, but it is not the shofar of the giving of the Torah — and this 'when the yovel sounds' was after the Tabernacle was erected, and then Moses blew the shofar and gave the people permission to ascend. Rav Saadia Gaon. Another interpretation: <b>when the yovel sounds</b> — as in 'he drew his hand against the mockers' (i.e., 'drawing' in the sense of prolonging). And it is a sign for the departure of the Shekhinah."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The phrase דחיה קודמת לסקילה refers to the mishnaic procedure in Sanhedrin where witnesses first push the condemned off a height, then stone him if he survives.", 2 "The second interpretation reads במשך as 'drawing out / prolonging' the horn-blast rather than 'when' it sounds."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:58:54.763005552Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}